[38;5;231m⏺[39m One more question that changes the data model, then I'll put the design together.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether a reminder time is stored [3mper task[0m or derived from one global rule. Per-task means a [38;5;153mremindAt[39m field
  and a UI control on every task; global means tasks store only [38;5;153mdueAt[39m and the reminder time is computed. Global is cheap
  to change later; going from global to per-task later means a data migration and new UI, so it's the more expensive
  direction.

  - [1mAt due time only[0m — simplest possible: [38;5;153mdueAt[39m fires the notification. No extra field, no extra UI. Costs: no warning,
    you hear about it exactly when it's already due.
  - [1mGlobal lead time[0m — one setting ("warn me 15 minutes before"), applied to every task. One field in settings, no
    per-task UI. Covers most real use with almost no complexity.
  - [1mPer-task reminder[0m — each task carries its own reminder time. Maximum control, a new field and a control in the task
    editor, and it's the version you'd want if different tasks genuinely need different warning.
  - [1mOverdue nagging[0m — re-notify on an interval until the task is done. Independent of the above; mostly an annoyance
    risk.

  [1mMy recommendation: at due time, plus a global lead-time setting.[0m It covers the realistic case with one settings value,
  and if you later find you want per-task control, adding a nullable [38;5;153mremindAt[39m that falls back to the global rule is a
  clean extension rather than a rewrite.

